fpc_product_dec_dict = {
    "Assignment of Receivables (AoR)": "An agreement between DLL and a third-party (e.g. vendor) in which the vendor assigns the receivable created under a contract between such third-party (vendor) and another party (e.g. end-user). DLL gets the right to collect the receivables from the end user but does not become liable for the performance of the obligations thereunder. In many situations DLL will not have title to or a pledge on assets being financed under the contract, increasing risk.",
    "Cross Border Product": "A financial arrangement, including leases and loans, that crosses national borders. Such financing introduces additional risks to the financing party including currency risk, political and sovereign risk, legal risk, tax related risks and collateral control.",
    "Financial Lease (FL)": "All of the risks and rewards incidental to ownership of the leased asset are transferred to the lessee. Features are (a) the present value of the minimum lease payments is more than 90% of the of the initial investment at inception of the lease; and/or (b) economically it is expected that the lessee will obtain ownership at the end of the term, by automatic purchase or bargain purchase option, indicating it is highly likely that the lessee will exercise his rights to purchase the asset. As finance lease, during the term of the lease, the title of the equipment sits with DLL and DLLs balance sheet for tax purposes. A right of use asset sits on the balance sheet of the lessee. A finance lease has no or very small RVs.",
    "Leasing Import": "A lease transaction for the acquisition of imported products in which due to the modality of Import By Order it becomes possible to include in the operation, beyond the value of the equipment, all costs of import, nationalization, taxes and transportation. In general terms, the End Customer negotiates the conditions of the equipment with the Manufacturer and instructs DLL to carry out the importation. DLL orders the imported equipment to Trading Company and Trading Company performs as the importer, carrying out the entire import leasing operation. DLL delivers the equipment to the End Customer and the leasing starts.",
    "Loans for Non-Business Customers": "Loans for Non-Business Customers is a transfer of funds from DLL to a third party that is classified as a consumer under local regulation, with the funds commonly used to fund an asset purchase. This product is currently only approved for use in the US and usage is limited to consumers, which are defined as natural persons who purchase the equipment for personal, family, or household use  and not business use. These loans are most commonly secured by collateral, such as a pledge over the asset, but can be unsecured. DLL may or may not have liability in the performance of the asset under a loan.",
    "Loans for Professional Customers": "A transfer of funds from DLL to a third-party business, with the funds commonly used to fund an asset purchase. Interest is added to the capital element and repaid over an agreed term. Loans can be secured by collateral, such as a pledge over the asset, or can be unsecured. DLL may or may not have liability in the performance of the asset under a loan.",
    "Operating Lease (OL)": "A contract wherein the owner (Lessor) permits the user (Lessee) to use of an asset for a particular period which is shorter than the economic life of the asset, without any transfer of ownership rights. There will always be a residual value exposure taken by the Lessor (which may or may not be fully or partially covered by a third party).  When a lease qualifies as an operating lease, it is measured at the original asset price and depreciated on a straight-line basis to the residual value estimated at the start of the lease. Accounting for the Lessee can vary and depends on the accounting framework applied by the Lessee (IFRS, US GAAP, Local GAAP).  As has always been the case  DLL never makes any representations regarding the on or off balance sheet character or treatment of a lease. This remains the sole responsibility of the Lessee.",
    "Sale & Lease Back (SLB)": "A Sale & Lease Back transaction occurs when DLL purchases equipment from a third-party (i.e. the customer) and then leases it back to the same third-party.",
    "Transfer of Contract (ToC)": "A structure in which all rights and obligations under that contract are transferred to DLL. Following such transfer DLL is a party to the transferred contract. DLL obtains rights to receive payments from the end-user, but DLL also becomes responsible for the contracted obligations to the end-user, including ongoing service obligations (if any). In common law countries a similar result is reached through a so called novation.",
    "Asset Based Lending (ABL)": "Asset Based Lending is a revolving credit facility with a borrower used to fund working capital needs. Credit available under the facility is determined and limited by the borrower's qualified assets (commonly focused in accounts receivable) at the time of borrowing. The lender retains control of all the borrower's cash via a bank lock box to which all receipts due the borrower are directed. The lender applies all lock box cash to the outstanding balance on the facility.",
    "Pay-as-Sold (PAS)": "PAS is an inventory finance loan that provides dealers the ability to finance products without repayment until the earlier of when the product is sold or the maturity date of the loan. Payment due at the time of sale is intended to match a dealer's cash flow. It offers dealers the ability to have inventory on hand and ready for sale when an end user customer is ready to buy. Dealers may or may not make scheduled principal payments (also called curtailments) to DLL, but the full balance of the loan is due the earlier when the product is been sold or the maturity of the financing, thus allowing for good cash flow control. Typically, this financing is asset specific and DLL needs to be able to identify a financed asset by serial number or unique identifier.",
    "Scheduled Payment Programs (SPP)": "SPP is an inventory finance product aimed at providing extended terms (generally less than 180 days but may be extended to up to 360 days depending on industry and underlying asset turns) to dealers for paying the purchased inventory, for resale to end users. SPP can be offered on an unsecured basis, usually supported by credit insurance or other appropriate mitigation elements. Suppliers generally support the extended terms with a subsidy to DLL, and maximum terms generally match the natural turn of the assets through the delivery channel. Because this product is generally targeted at smaller-ticket, faster-turn assets, repayment dates are calculated as a specific number of days from the manufacturer invoice date rather than being tied to the dealers ultimate sale date of the asset.",
    "Short-Term Finance": "A commercial facility providing short-term finance to finance end user's purchases of replacement parts and service work, supplied by the dealer network (or manufacturer). The finance is for either principal and interest payments but can be subsidized no interest/no payments programs.",
    "Asset Trading": "Equipment Trading is the activity of purchasing and selling equipment for a profit. The sourcing of equipment or assets can be from  DLL Inventory (Assets in Stock), vendors, other banks, auctions, and other sales forums.",
    "Pay-per-use": "Pay-per-Use is a product where payment from the customer to DLL is based on the usage of the underlying asset by the customer or the performance / output that is delivered to the customer and where the financial return to DLL is variable.",
    "Servitization Bucket 2": "Bundled lease with Service & Maintenance ranging from all-in contract with standard industry standard services (Bucket 2), plus additional optional services relating to the asset such as wear parts, support, uptime guarantees (Bucket 3) to full-service contracts that also include other services not directly related to the assets such as fleet management, cost savings, efficiency (Bucket 4). Each bucket is based on an all-in contract consisting of a lease part and a non-lease part, both included on one invoice.",
    "Servitization Bucket 3": "Bundled lease with Service & Maintenance ranging from all-in contract with standard industry standard services (Bucket 2), plus additional optional services relating to the asset such as wear parts, support, uptime guarantees (Bucket 3) to full-service contracts that also include other services not directly related to the assets such as fleet management, cost savings, efficiency (Bucket 4). Each bucket is based on an all-in contract consisting of a lease part and a non-lease part, both included on one invoice.",
    "Servitization Bucket 4": "Bundled lease with Service & Maintenance ranging from all-in contract with standard industry standard services (Bucket 2), plus additional optional services relating to the asset such as wear parts, support, uptime guarantees (Bucket 3) to full-service contracts that also include other services not directly related to the assets such as fleet management, cost savings, efficiency (Bucket 4). Each bucket is based on an all-in contract consisting of a lease part and a non-lease part, both included on one invoice.",
    "Floorplan new / used": "Asset-based inventory finance for new or used equipment, either with the supplier of goods involved (program business) or directly with the dealer (dealer direct). Purpose is to bridge the gap between purchase and sale of an an individual asset, i.e. equipment financed is stock and will be repaid once being sold. ",
    "Floorplan Demo": "Same as Floorplan new/ used, but dealers have the option to hand over the equipment to potential buyers for demonstration purposes.",
    "Rental Fleet Financing": "Flexible short-term financing of equipment in a rental fleet. Assets can be sold at any time, as the related financing can be terminated without break cost.",
    "Input or Consumables Finance": "Inventory finance for (agricultural) inputs or consumables, i. e. low unit value goods which cannot be identified individually. Financing tenor should correspond to projected inventory turn ratio. ",
    "Parts Finance": "Inventory finance for spare parts or accessories, i. e. low unit value goods which cannot be identified individually. Financing tenor should correspond to projected inventory turn ratio. ",
}

